														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2344652">
									

<p>Pros:</p>
<ul>
<li>I like the characterisation. It encompasses aspects of ukiyo (floating world).</li>
<li>I like the translated poems. While they feel haiku-ish, they do not adhere to the supposed format. But then again, these are translations of Japanese. I will leave it to those versed in Japanese to judge if they can pass off as a legit Japanese style of poetry (be it haiku, waka or tanka).</li>
<li>EDIT: In light of Kalinin's explanation, I voice my enjoyment of a non-intrusive Foundation as an unique feature. My con (addressing the lacklustre procedures) is still valid though. It's more of me buying the spirit of it, than its implementation here.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cons:</p>
<ul>
<li>While the effect is limited to a range, I don't really see the relevance of the procedures. Why not vacate all houses within the area of effect and monitor the people whose deaths are predicted?</li>
</ul>
<p>Verdict: Upvote</p>
<p>Side note: While the interview format used here is &quot;unconventional&quot;, I'm not bothered by it since it's clear who is the interviewer and interviewee.</p>

