<p>In regards to the poetry, it's unavoidable (unless the author went out of the way to get expert opinion). Anyway, as English poems wrapped in a Japanese form, they are acceptable works of poetry.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not going to go too far into what ultimately will be a difference in how I perceive this iteration of the Foundation, but my thought is that the Foundation views a woman from the local &quot;university&quot; coming to visit a harmless old eccentric once a month or so being far less conspicuous and disruptive (and less cost-intensive) than mass relocation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unconventional indeed, but I can respect that. Normalcy may be best preserved if there are no real observable alterations to one's daily lives (be it anomalous or non-anomalous). The villagers are used to an old man predicting deaths, then so be it.</p>
<p>I do enjoy a non-intrusive take on the Foundation.</p>

