			<div class="content" id="post-content-2345043">
									

<blockquote>
<p>That said, I do dislike the minimalist interview for the sake of fitting the word count.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Point of order. There's a reason the interview is the way it is for a narrative purpose, not word count.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;long&nbsp;winded&nbsp;explanation</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;long&nbsp;winded&nbsp;explanation</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The researcher that is conducting the interview has included her notes, not an interview transcript, into the file. This is for several reasons. She did not bring a recording device, so she is essentially transcribing her notes from the encounter. She also is not in the habit of recording extraneous information, due to her personality. She focuses on what she considers the important points of something and excludes all else.</p>
<p>This is why, by the way, she has been assigned to this SCP. The Foundation, for reasons I sort of went into already, is not a prison in this universe unless it absolutely must be. Chances are, an old guy who keeps to himself in a mountainous, remote fishing village in rural Japan is not going to be threatening consensus reality anytime soon. So they put some minimal safeguards in place, and containment and research is largely left to a researcher assigned to build trust with the subject and to generally keep tabs on things. Dr. Todoroki was chosen because this Foundation makes a practice of assigning researchers for human subjects based on how well they will be able to work with them. Mikio and Dr. Todoroki share a certain outlook, which is why the interview gets a little informal at times.</p>
<p>It also is an intentional choice on my part to stay on a theme of minimalism, which was one I chose based on the contest, but which I made narrative choices in service to, rather than an arbitrary word count. The skip is a minimal threat. The man lives a simple life. His friends leave brief monuments. The researcher sticks to the important points. Etc.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>As always, if it doesn't work, then it doesn't work for you. That's fine. But the interview is a stylistic choice, not an attempt to shoehorn something in under the limit. It always looked that way, even in earlier, longer drafts.</p>

							</div>

								
