														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2345059">
									

<p>This is probably my favorite skip of the contest. Both the anomaly and the brevity play into each other to make something really wonderful, the Foundation's low-key, minimalistic (yet effective containment) works wonderfully, and the story plays out in a way that, despite bordering on a tale, works perfectly fine in the SCP format. This isn't a case of the SCP format limiting the narrative at all, even if a tale based on this guy would be wonderful.</p>
<p>What really gets me is the interview. Todoroki and Mikio interact with each other as if they're friends, yet still have to be formal about the whole affair. There's humanity in this that is so scarce throughout most of the other mainlisters on the site and it's wonderful to read about. And it's believable, too.</p>
<p>One thing I noticed, and I'm not sure if this is intentional due to these poems being translated into English, but some of the lines don't exactly fit the format. I'm not sure if you wrote them in Japanese first and then translated, or just wrote the English first.</p>

							</div>

								
					
